.\" Manpage for pairmux.
.\" Written in roff; kept concise and regenerated by hand alongside releases.
.TH PAIRMUX 1 "2026-07-18" "pairmux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pairmux \- reliable terminal primitives for AI agents on tmux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pairmux
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR SOCKET ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pairmux
is an Agent\-Computer Interface (ACI) layer built on top of
.BR tmux (1).
It gives AI agents a reliable, blocking interface for driving real terminals
while humans can watch, attach, and help at any time. Each managed terminal is a
tmux pane whose raw output is journaled; pairmux detects command completion
(via OSC 133 shell integration, a sentinel fallback, or output quiescence),
shapes the output for models (strips ANSI, folds carriage returns, truncates
with an explicit follow\-up command), and reports structured status. Adding
.B \-\-json
to any command emits a machine\-readable envelope; without it the output is
human\-friendly text.
.B pairmux
requires
.BR tmux (1)
3.2 or newer.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR new " [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-cwd\fR \fID\fR] [\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fICMD\fR]"
Create a managed terminal (a tmux pane with journaling and shell hooks) and
print its name.
.TP
.BR run " \fINAME\fR \fICMD\fR.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDUR\fR]"
Send a command and block until it finishes or times out, returning a structured
result. A timeout is not a failure: the reply tells the agent how to keep
waiting.
.TP
.BR peek " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-screen\fR | \fB\-\-tail\fR \fIN\fR]"
Show recent output and current status immediately, without waiting.
.TP
.BR wait " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-idle\fR \fIMS\fR | \fB\-\-pattern\fR \fIRE\fR | \fB\-\-human\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDUR\fR]"
Block until a condition is met: output goes idle, a pattern appears, or a human
finishes helping.
.TP
.BR send " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-text\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-key\fR \fIK\fR] [\fB\-\-enter\fR]"
Send raw input to an interactive program (REPL, TUI, or confirmation prompt).
.TP
.BR log " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fIN\fR | \fB\-\-grep\fR \fIRE\fR | \fB\-\-range\fR \fIA:B\fR]"
Retrieve the full or filtered journal, resolving truncation and scrollback.
.TP
.B ls
List managed terminals with their status, current command, and last activity.
.TP
.BR kill " \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR"
Kill one or all terminals while keeping their journals on disk.
.TP
.BR attach " [\fINAME\fR]"
Hand control to a human by attaching to the underlying tmux session.
.TP
.BR watch " [\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIDUR\fR]"
Show a live dashboard of all terminals; Ctrl\-C quits.
.TP
.BR note " \fINAME\fR \fITEXT\fR.\|.\|."
Leave a message in the journal for the agent (or another human) to see next.
.TP
.B doctor
Probe the environment: tmux version, shell\-integration hooks, and journal
writability.
.TP
.B version
Print the pairmux version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PAIRMUX_STATE_DIR
Override the state directory (journals and index). Defaults to
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/pairmux
or
.IR ~/.local/state/pairmux .
.TP
.B PAIRMUX_SOCKET
Use a specific tmux socket (equivalent to the
.B \-\-socket
flag).
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base directory for state when
.B PAIRMUX_STATE_DIR
is unset.
.TP
.B SHELL
Selects the shell\-integration hooks (bash, zsh, or fish) injected into new
terminals.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/pairmux/<terminal>/raw.log
Raw journal of a terminal's output bytes (mode 0600).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/pairmux/<terminal>/index.jsonl
Structured index of command boundaries, notes, and human\-attach events.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1)
.SH AUTHOR
treeleaves30760
